<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>Strategies to Support Mathematical Processes</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00019</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Strategies to Support Mathematical Processes</md:title>
    <md:uuid>790fcee5-15ea-46b2-9069-40f318874f09</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Content-specific instructional strategies, labeled “Support for Students with Disabilities,” are included in each lesson. They are designed to help teachers meet the individual needs of a diverse group of learners. Each is aligned to one of the<emphasis> three principles of Universal Design for Learning</emphasis>, to provide multiple means of <emphasis>engagement</emphasis>, <emphasis>representation</emphasis>, or <emphasis>action and expression</emphasis>, and includes a suggested strategy to increase access and eliminate barriers.</para>
<para>These lesson-specific supports can help students succeed with a specific activity without reducing the task's mathematical demand. They can also fade out as students gain understanding and fluency.</para>
<para>The following areas of cognitive functioning are integral to learning mathematics (Addressing Accessibility project, Brodesky et al., 2002). They are indicated in each access support so that teachers can leverage students’ strengths to address potential barriers or challenges in the learning environment. These areas align and supplement the <link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/918c44350d9c3c4ba6d39d8d91e2ea936b630d62">TEA Supporting Strategies for Learning Abilities</link>.</para>
<table class="os-raise-textheavytable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>Universal Design for Learning</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>TEA Supporting Strategies for 
        Learning Abilities</emphasis></entry></row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry><para><emphasis>Conceptual Processing</emphasis> includes perceptual reasoning, problem solving, and metacognition.</para></entry>
      <entry><para>Thinking &amp; Reasoning</para></entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><para><emphasis>Language</emphasis> includes auditory and visual language processing and expression.</para></entry>
      <entry><para>Vocabulary Knowledge</para>
        </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><para><emphasis>Visual-Spatial Processing</emphasis> includes processing visual information and understanding relation in space of visual mathematical representations and geometric concepts.</para></entry>
      <entry><para>Visual Spatial Reasoning</para></entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><para><emphasis>Organization</emphasis> includes organizational skills, attention, and focus.</para></entry>
      <entry><para>Processing Speed</para>
        </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><para><emphasis>Memory</emphasis> includes working memory and short-term memory.</para></entry>
      <entry><para>Working Memory</para>
        <para>Efficiency of Learning</para>
        <para>Retrieval of Information</para></entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><para><emphasis>Attention</emphasis> includes paying attention to details, maintaining focus, and filtering out extraneous information.</para></entry>
      <entry/>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><para><emphasis>Social-Emotional Functioning</emphasis> includes interpersonal skills and the cognitive comfort and safety required to take risks and make mistakes.</para></entry>
      <entry/>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><para><emphasis>Fine Motor Skills </emphasis>includes tasks that require small muscle movement and coordination such as manipulating objects (graphing, cutting with scissors, writing).</para></entry>
      <entry/>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<para>Additional support for students with disabilities was designed using the Universal Design for Learning Guidelines (http://udlguidelines.cast.org). Each support aligns with one of the three principles of UDL: engagement, representation, and action and expression.</para>
<title>Differentiation in the Math Classroom</title>
<para>The following resources were collected to sample teaching and learning strategies that are especially beneficial in the math classroom broadly and for Algebra I specifically. For more<emphasis> “Inclusion in Texas”</emphasis> support strategies from the Texas Education Agency (TEA) to add to your library of resources, please <link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/918c44350d9c3c4ba6d39d8d91e2ea936b630d62">download this pdf</link>.</para>

<para>TR= Thinking and reasoning</para>
<para>PS = Processing speed</para>
<para>WM= Working memory</para>
<para>EoL= Efficiency of learning</para>
<para>RoL= Retrieval of information</para>
<para>VK= Vocabulary knowledge</para>
<para>VSP= Visual Spatial Reasoning</para>


<table class="os-raise-textheavyadjustedtable">
  <caption>
  Inclusion for Abilities 
